the_loop:
Every method in this explainer runs some version of this loop.

Step 0, start. Begin from a pretrained policy that already succeeds sometimes: a BC diffusion or flow policy, ACT, or a VLA such as pi0, pi0.5 or OpenVLA-OFT. If it never succeeds (0%), most methods have nothing to climb. SimpleVLA-RL stays at 0 -> 0 on RoboTwin 2.0 from a 0-demo SFT.

Step 1, deploy. Run the policy to collect episodes. They can come from the real robot, a fleet, a GPU simulator, a scanned or generated digital twin (the sim-to-real family), or a learned action-conditioned video world model (the world-model family). Each has a cost: real episodes need resets and supervision, and simulated ones can be wrong in ways the policy will exploit.

Step 2, measure. Turn each episode into a number. The options are a binary success check (a human keypress, a hand-written detector, a classifier), a dense progress or value model (the reward family), or a VLM judge. For comparing checkpoints, you want an evaluator that ranks policies the same way reality does, reported as Pearson r and MMRV against paired real trials (the world-model/evaluation family). Offline action MSE is a poor proxy: SIMPLER found average Pearson r 0.308, vs 0.924 for visual-matching sim.

Step 3, find failures. Locate where and why the policy goes wrong. The tools are dips in a progress model (Dyna), runtime monitors, either calibrated on successful runs (CoRe) or triggered by jumps in the critic's Q (JITI), human takeovers (the HITL family), VLM diagnosis of failed videos (F4R, PDE), and per-stage predicates (Temporal GRPO). This step decides where the next batch of data, search or corrections goes.

Step 4, improve. Apply an update. Each family is a different update operator:
- black-box search perturbs a small parameter set (gains, a noise vector, a recipe) and keeps what scores better;
- on-policy PPO/GRPO raises the log-probability of rollouts that beat a baseline;
- off-policy actor-critic learns Q from every stored transition and pushes actions toward higher Q;
- residual, edit and steering methods train a small add-on around a frozen base;
- advantage-weighted or advantage-conditioned BC keeps the supervised loss but chooses what to copy;
- DAgger-style methods train on human (or machine) corrections at the states the policy actually reaches;
- test-time search changes no weights: it samples candidates and picks with a verifier or guides the sampler with a critic's gradient.

Step 5, re-measure. Evaluate on held-out episodes and start states that were not used for search or training. Report trial counts and confidence intervals, and track speed, intervention rate and per-task results, not just average success. Then repeat. Many loops also distill what worked back into the base (PLD, VERITAS); Q-Planning instead refits only its critic each round and leaves the policy frozen. Others restart each round from the pretrained checkpoint to avoid drift (RECAP; DEED regressed from 42% to 22% in round 2).

How the other families plug in:
- reward and value models serve steps 2-3 for everyone else;
- world models and twins make steps 1-2 cheap;
- HITL methods put a person into steps 3-4;
- industry recipes are mostly this loop built as infrastructure: fleet logging, auto-grading, targeted retraining.
